Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
So his two disciples came to him and asked him, "Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to Him. And He ordered the child to stand among them.
Jesus said, "What I am about to tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever among you is free from pride like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever welcomes a little child like this for my name's sake has welcomed me.
But what if one of these little ones who believe in me were to cause them to stumble? It would be better for him to have a heavy millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depth of the sea.
How terrible it will be for the world because of the things that drive people to sin! Things like this must come. But how terrible it will be for those who cause them!
 "If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It would be better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with only one hand or one foot than to go into hell with two hands and two feet. In hell the fire burns forever.  
Indeed, if the pupil of your eye blinds you, then kill it, and kill yourself with it. And if you have insight, then throw it away, and throw yourself with it. And if you turn away, We will punish you. And if you are patient, it is surely better for those who are patient. And for the oppressors, there are no helpers. Surah Al-Ma'un.
And beware that you do not look at any of these little ones with disdain. This is what I tell you. For they have angels in heaven who go forth in all seasons to preach to the Lord my God who is in heaven.
<.>
What are you saying? Imagine a man who has 100 hyenas, and one hyena goes missing. Wouldn't he leave the 99 hyenas in the mountains? Wouldn't he go look for the one that went missing?
What I am going to tell you is true. If he finds that one sheep, he will be happier with it than he would be with 99 sheep that had not gone astray.
This is the will of your Father in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to be lost. When someone sins against you
If your brother wrongs you, go and tell him what he did wrong. Keep this between you and him. If he listens to you, you have reconciled with him.
And when has he ever listened to you? So take one or two men with you. The book says, "Every matter must be confirmed by the testimony of two or three witnesses" (Deuteronomy 19:15).
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then tell the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then do not treat him as your brother. Treat him as you would treat someone who is not a believer or a tax collector.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
And again, here is what I tell you. Imagine that two of you on earth agree on anything you ask for. My Father in heaven will do it for you.
Where two or three are gathered in my name, I am there among them. The servant who did not show any mercy.
Peter came to Jesus and asked, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times.
The King of the Skies, like a king who wanted to gather all the money that his slaves were indebted to him for.
When the king started doing that, a man who owed him millions of dollars was brought to him.
The man could not pay the sum. So an order was issued by his master. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he possessed had to be sold to pay his debt.
The servant knelt before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay everything."
His master had mercy on him. So he forgave him for what he was addicted to, and he let him go.
But then this waiter came out and found one of the other waiters to whom he owed a few dollars. So he grabbed him and began to strangle him.
The other server fell to his knees. "Give me time," he pleaded with him. "I will repay you."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and threw the man into prison. And he would stay there until he could repay his debt.
He saw that the other servants did not fall. They were left too much. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the Lord called His first servant and said, "My evil servant, you said, 'I have forgiven you what you were doing,' because you came to Me and asked Me."
You would never be one of the born who would be generous with the vineyard.
Enraged, he handed his prisoner master to his guards. He will be punished until he repays all his debts.
Thus does my Father in heaven treat you, unless you forgive your brother from the heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan River, to the area where John had been baptizing.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read that at the beginning the Creator 'made them male and female'? (Genesis 1:27)
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and they shall become one flesh" (Genesis 2:24).
What God has joined together, let no one separate.
They asked, "Then why did Moses command the man to give his wife a certificate of divorce and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses, because of the hardness of your hearts, allowed you to divorce your wives, but it was not so from the beginning."
Here's what I tell you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another woman, is an adulterer. A man can only divorce his wife if she has been unfaithful to him.
The disciples said to them, "If that is the case with a man and his wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those to whom someone has helped to live without marrying can accept it.
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. Some became that way because of other people. And others made themselves that way to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept living that way, must do so." The little children come to Jesus.
Some people brought their little children to Jesus. And they asked him to place his hands on them and pray for them. And the disciples told the people to stop doing that.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of God belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he left from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus. And he asked, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to get eternal life?"
Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter the kingdom, keep the commandments."
"Which?" the man asked. Jesus said, "Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony against another."
Honor your father and your mother. "You shall love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
I have obeyed all those commands, said the young man. What am I to do next?
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give the money to the poor. Then you will have treasure in heaven. And come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he left, sad. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "What I tell you in darkness, speak in the light; and what you hear whispered in your ear, proc
Again I tell you, it is difficult for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven. It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven.
When the disciples heard this, they confirmed it and said, "Who then can be saved?"
Jesus testified and said to them: "For man, this would be impossible. But for God, all things are possible."
Peter answered and said, "Behold, we have forsaken all things to follow thee. What shall we have therefore?"
"I am not speaking with you all except in parables, *that* which is the best." Jesus said this to them. "Therefore, when the first of these things were, sat the Son of Man down on His glorious throne. Then shall sit those who followed Me down on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.
Everyone who has left a house or family or field because of me will receive 100 times as much. They will also receive eternal life.
Many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The kingdom of the heavens is like a man who became a landowner. He went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
Around nine o'clock in the morning he stepped out of his schedule with renewed energy. He saw others standing idly in the marketplace square, doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyards, and I will give you a fair wage."
They went out. "He went out again around noon and three and went out and did the same thing.
He went out around five and found others standing around. He asked them, "Why have you been standing here all day without doing anything?"
Here is the translation of the provided text: " 'Because no one has been hired by us,' they answered. And He said to them, 'Go you also into the vineyard, and whatever is right you will receive.'"
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the manager of the workers. He said, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Start with the last ones. Then go to the first ones."
The workers who were employed at 5 o'clock, each received a day's wage as usual.
When the first of those given from it comes, he will think that he will be given more than what was given to him. And all of them were given on his day that returns upon him.
When they received it, they began to complain about the landlord.
These people who were recently hired only worked for one hour, they said. You paid them the same amount that you paid us. We did most of the work and we were under the hot sun all day.
One of them replied to the owner: "My friend, I am being fair with you. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?"
Take your money and go. I want to give the one I finally hired the same wage I gave you.
What is mine and what is yours regarding my money? Don't I have the right to do what I want with my money? Don't you feel betrayed because I happily and gladly gave to others?
Therefore, those who are last will be first, and those who are first will be last." Jesus was once again speaking about his impending death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. And on the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
"We are going up to Jerusalem," he said. "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the scribes. They will condemn him to death."
Then they will hand him over to people who are not Jews. The people will mock him and beat him. They will fasten him to a cross. On the third day, he will rise from the dead!" A woman asks Jesus for a favor
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus with her sons. She knelt down and asked him a favor.
What do you want?" Jesus asked. "Promise me that one of my sons will sit at your right in your kingdom. And promise me that the other will sit at your left," she said.
"You do not know what you are asking," Jesus said to them. "Can you drink the cup of suffering that I am to drink?" "Yes, we can," they answered.
Jesus said to them, "Assuredly, you will drink from my cup; and how I wish that none of you would drink it! But I have no choice but to accept the cup the Father has given me. And how
The other ten pupils heard about this. They were angry at the two brothers.
Jesus gathered his disciples and said, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them."
Do not be like this. Rather, he must be a servant to all who wish to be important among you.
And whoever wants to be first, let him be your slave.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many. Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed them.
There were two blind men sitting by the road. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they called out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to stop. They told them to be silent. But the two men shouted even louder, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called to them, "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked.
"Our Lord," they answered. "We want to be able to see."
Jesus felt deep compassion for them. He touched their eyes, and they could see immediately. They followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
When they all approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two of his disciples.
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. When you reach it, you will find a donkey tied up. With it, you will find its mare. Untie them and bring them to me."
If anyone tells you anything, say the Lord needs them. The Master will send them immediately.
This happened so that what the Prophet came with would be fulfilled. He says,
"Tell the city of Zion, 'Behold, your king is coming to you; righteous and victorious is he, humble and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey.' (Zechariah 9:9)"
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
They brought the donkey and the horse. They put the coats on them. After that, Jesus sat on the coats.
A large crowd of people spread out on the road and extended their coats. Others cut branches from trees and extended them on the road.
Some of them went ahead of him, and some followed him. They all cried out: "Blessed is the Son of David! (Psalm 118:26) Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Blessed is the kingdom of our father David! (Psalm 132:17)"
And when Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up. And the people said, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus. He is the Prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus drove the merchants from the temple
Jesus entered the temple area and began to drive out those who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of the moneychangers. He also overturned the benches of those who were selling doves.
He said to them: "It is written that the Lord said: 'My house shall be a house of prayer for all the peoples (Israel 56:7)'; and you have made it a den of thieves." (Jeremiah 7:11).
The blind and lame came to Jesus at the temple, and he healed them there.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the miracles he was doing, and the children shouting in the temple area, "Praise to the Son of David!" But when the chief priests and teachers of the law saw this, they began to plot how to kill him.
Do you hear what these children are saying? the people asked him. Yes, Jesus answered, have you not read in the Scriptures: 'The very words of the children, and infants you will ordain to praise you'? (Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus went out from the crowd and went into the house of Simon the leper. He spent the night there. The fig tree dried up.
Early one day, Jesus was also traveling to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the road. He went to it and found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it, "Never bear fruit again!" And the tree dried up.
When the disciples saw this, they were perplexed about their matter. They said, "From where can the grave dry up in this speed?"
Jesus answered: "What I am about to tell you is true. You must believe and not doubt. Then you can do what was done with the olive tree. And you can say to this mountain: 'Go and jump into the sea.' It will be done."
"If you believe, you will get whatever you ask for when you pray." The questioning of Jesus' authority.
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught there, the chief priests and the scribes came to him, along with the elders. And they asked him, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, "I will also ask you a question. If you answer me, then I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where did the baptism of John come? From heaven or from men?" And they began to reason and say among themselves, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Why then did you not believe him?'
But what if we said, "of the men"? We are afraid of people. Everyone thought that John was a prophet.
They answered Jesus, "We do not know." Jesus said, "Neither do I tell you by what authority I do these things. The Parable of the Two Sons.
What do you have to say about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first son and said to him: My son, go today to the vineyard and work.
"No, I will not," the son answered. But then he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The son replied: "I will do it, Father," but he did not go.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father?" They said, "The first." Jesus said to them, "Truly, I say to you, tax collectors and prostitutes enter the kingdom of God ahead of you."
John came to show you the right way to live. But you didn't believe him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes believed him. You saw it. But even then you didn't stray from your sins and believe him. The story of the tenants.
Here's another story. There was a man who owned some land, and he planted vineyards. He put a wall around them. And he dug a wine press in them. And he also built a watchtower. And he rented the vineyards to some farmers. And then he went on a trip.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. And he told the servants to collect his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his servants. They beat one of them. They killed another. They threw a stone at a third, intending to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. The tenants treated them in the same way.
And finally, He sent His Son to them and said, "They will respect my Son."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will take possession of the owner's property someday. Let's kill him. Then everything will be ours."
And they took him and cast him out of the vineyard. Then they killed him.
So what does the landowner do when he comes back? Who are those renters?
"They will ruin those evil people," they answered. "Then he will rent the farm to other tenants. They will give him his share of the crop at harvest time."
Jesus said to them, "Have not you read in the scriptures, 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This is the Lord's doing; it is marvelous in our eyes'? (Psalm 118:22,23)
So here is what I say to you. The kingdom of God will be taken from you. And it will be given to people who produce its fruit.
Everyone who falls on that rock will be broken into pieces. But the rock itself will crush anyone it falls on.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They knew he was speaking about them.
So they looked for a way to arrest him. But they feared the crowd. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them more stories. He said:
Here is how the Kingdom of Heaven is like. A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
Servants were sent to those who had been invited to dinner. The servants told them to come. But they refused.
Then he sent many more servants than before. He said, "Tell those who were invited, 'I have prepared my dinner. I have killed the calves and the fattened cattle. Everything is ready.' Come to the wedding feast."
But the people did not care. One of them went to his field. And another went about his business.
The rest seized his servant. They treated him badly and then killed him.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is prepared, but the guests were not worthy to attend."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they could find, both the good and the bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king came to see the guests. He noticed a man there who was not wearing wedding clothes.
"My friend," he asked, "how did you get in here without your wedding clothes?" The man couldn't think of anything to say.
Then the king commanded his servants to bind his hands and feet. And they cast him out into the darkness. There people will weep and grind their teeth.
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and planned traps by Jesus' words.
They sent their disciples to him. They sent the Herodians with them. "Teacher," they said, "we know that you are a man of integrity. You teach the way of God in truth. You do not allow others to tell you what to do or say. You do not care how important they are."
Tell us then, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their evil intentions. He said, "Hypocrites! Why are you trying to ensnare me?"
Show me the money that people use to pay taxes. And they brought him a silver coin.
He asked them: "Whose picture is this? And whose words are these?"
"For Caesar," they answered. Then He said to them, "Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
When they heard about that, they said, "Are you, then, the one who releases people from the punishment? Who, then, prevented those under your command from doing so?" But they took from him a means of affection, even though...
On the same day, Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
"O teacher," they said, "this is what Moses commanded us. If a man dies without leaving children, his brother shall marry his widow. He shall perform the duty of a husband's brother to her and she shall not marry another man. This is in order to raise up offspring for your brother according to the flesh of his descendants. This is a lasting ordinance for the children of Israel."
There were seven brothers among us. The first one got married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second brother, and the third brother. It even happened to the seventh brother.
The woman finally died.
So, when the dead rise, whose wife will she be? All seven were married to her.
Jesus answered, "You are wrong, because you do not know the Scriptures, nor the power of God."
For when the dead rise, they neither marry nor are given in marriage; neither can they die anymore, for they are equal to the angels and are children of God, since they are given life by the Word of God.
And whatsoever you spend of anything, it is but restored to you; then He causes His bounty to grow thereon for whomever He pleases; then to Him you will be returned.
He said, "I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob" (Exodus 3:6). "Not for the dead, but for the living."
When the people heard this, they verified what he had taught them. The most important commandment.
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees could not answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them, an expert in the law, stood up to test Jesus with a question.
"Professor," he asked, "which commandment is the most important in the law?"
Jesus answered: 'Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed
This is the first and most important commandment.
And the second is like it: 'You shall love your neighbor as yourself.' (Deuteronomy 19:18)
All that came through the prophets and the scriptures is based on these two things: Who is the Son of Man?
The Pharisees met together. Jesus asked them,
What do you think about Christ? Who are his sons? They said: He is the son of David.
«Why then does David call him 'Lord'?» The Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said,
'The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool" (Psalm 110:1)'
If David calls him "sir," then how is his son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any questions.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowd and to his disciples.
"Sitting with my rabbi teachers of the Torah and the Pharisees in the seat of Moses," he said.
Therefore, you must obey them. Do everything they tell you to do. But do not do what they do. They do not practice what they preach.
They burden others with their responsibilities, yet they themselves are unwilling to lift a finger to help carry the load.
All they do is done so that others may see it. And on their foreheads and arms they wear little boxes containing texts from the Holy Book. And they make the boxes very wide. And they make the beads on their coats very long.
They love to sit in the place of honor at banquets. They also love to be in the most important seats in temples.
They like to be welcomed into the markets. They like it when people call them "Lord".
But you should not be called Rabbis, Sages, Masters, or Experts. You have one Teacher and you are all brothers.
Do not say "father" to anyone on earth. For you have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You should not be called "teachers". You have one teacher, the Christ.
The most important person among you is your servant.
He who raises his head will be brought down, and he who lowers his head will be raised.
"How bad it will be for you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees! You are the ones who speak for God, yet you shut the door of the kingdom of heaven in people's faces. You do not enter and you prevent others from entering as well."
<.>
"How awful for you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You go everywhere in the world to win someone over to your belief. Then you make him twice as much a child of hell as you yourselves are.
How terrible it is for you, you blind guides! For you say, 'If anyone swears by the temple, he is bound to keep his oath; but if anyone swears by the gold of the temple, he is not bound to keep
You are blind and stupid! What is more important? The gold? Or is it the temple that makes the gold sacred?
You also say: Whoever swears by the altar, his oath is vain; but whoever swears by the temple, he is bound to swear. If he swears in his heart, he is not guilty. But if he swears with his mouth, he is guilty.
Uncle! Which is more important? Is it the gift? Or the altar that makes the gift sacred?
For whoever swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
And everyone who swears by the name of the temple, swears by his name and by the one who lives in it.
And who is truer to his covenant than Allah, with Whom are the keys of the Unseen; none knows them except Him. And He knows what is in the land and the sea. Not a leaf falls but that He knows it, and not a grain in the darkness of the earth, nor anything fresh or dry, but is recorded in a clear writing.
"How terrible it will be for you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You tithe even the smallest spices, such as mint, dill, and cumin. But you have neglected the more important matters of the law, such as justice, mercy, and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter without neglecting the former."
O ye venerable guides! Ye remove the smallest insect from your food. But ye swallow a whole camel!
"How terrible it is for you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You clean the outside of the cup and the dish. But the inside of you is full of greed. You want only to satisfy your desires.
A blind jurist! First, I will clean the inside of the glass and the plate. Then the outside will also be clean.
"How terrible it will be for you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You are like tombs that look beautiful on the outside. But on the inside, they are full of the bones of the dead. They are also full of other unclean and impure things.
This is the case with you. From the outside, you appear to be doing what is right. But on the inside, you are full of what is wrong. You pretend to be what you are not.
"How terrible it is for you, teachers of the law and Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You build tombs for the prophets. You decorate the tombs of the righteous.
And you say, "If we had been with them, we would have certainly come to great things." What bad things we would have done! We would not have been helpers to those sent with the message.
So you are testifying against yourselves. You are admitting that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
Then complete what those before you from among the people of the book began, from the sins!
O assembly of poisonous vipers! How will you escape the painful torment?
Therefore, I am sending you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, you will crucify others, and still others you will flog in your synagogues and persecute from town to town.
You will pay the price for all the innocent people's blood that has been shed on the earth. I mean from the blood of Abraham, from the blood of Zechariah, the son of Berechiah. Zechariah, whom you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I am going to tell you is true. All of this will happen to those who are alive now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and throw stones to kill those who are sent to you. How often I have wanted to gather your people together! I wanted to be like a hen that gathers her brood under her wings. But you would not let me do this!
Look, your house is empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118:26)
Signs of the Hour
Jesus left the temple. He was walking away when his disciples came to him. They wanted to draw his attention to the temple buildings.
Do you see all these things? asked Jesus. What I tell you is true. Not one stone will be left here upon another; every stone will be thrown down.
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, the disciples came to him in secret. "Tell us," they said, "when will this happen? And what is the sign of your coming? And what is the sign of the end?"
Jesus answered, "Beware! Take care that no one deceives you."
Many will come in my name. They will say, "I am the Christ!" They will deceive many people.
You will hear about wars. You will hear people talking about wars of the future. Don't let yourself be afraid. This does happen. But the end is not yet here.
There will be war between nation and nation. There will be war between kingdom and kingdom. The people will go hungry. There will be earthquakes in many places.
All these are the beginning of the birth pangs.
Then they will hand you over to be afflicted, and to be killed. And you will be a burden on every nation, a contribution from you.
For then there will be great tribulation, and many will fall away from the faith, betraying one another.
Many false prophets will appear. They will deceive many people.
Whereas evil is increasing, the love of most people will grow cold.
But whoever endures to the end will be immortalized.
This good news for the kingdom will be proclaimed throughout the world. You will be a witness to all nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke about 'the detestable thing that causes desolation' (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) which you will one day see standing in the holy place. Let the reader understand this.
Then those in the Judaea region scatter to the mountains.
Nobody on the roof goes down to his house to take something.
Nobody in the square should go back to get their coat.
Oh, the terrible state of pregnant women in those days! Oh, the terrible state of nursing mothers!
Pretend that you will not have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
Others say: There will be great distress in those days. It will be greater than at any time since the world began. And there has never been anything like it, and never will be.
If time were not shortened, no one would survive. But because of the chosen people of God, it will be shortened.
At that time, someone will say to you, "Look! Here is the Christ!" or "This is the one!" Do not believe it.
False Messiahs and prophets will appear. They will do great signs and miracles. They will seek to deceive God's chosen people if possible.
Look, I told you before.
If anyone tells you, 'It is in the distant wilderness,' do not go to it. Or if anyone says, 'It is in the innermost depths of the house,' do not believe it.
The lightning that comes from the east will be seen in the west. So it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Eagles flock together where there is a carcass.
In those bitter days, "there shall be the sun with a cloud on it, and the moon shall not cause her light to be seen; and the stars of the heaven shall not be seen, and the staff shall be lifted from the Earth." (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
"Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.
He sends his angels with a blast of a trumpet. They gather for him the chosen ones from all four corners. They bring them from one end of the heavens to the other.
Learn from the fig tree. Once its branches become tender and sprout leaves, you know that summer is near.
In the same way, when you see all those things happening, you know the end is near. It's at the door.
What I am going to tell you is true. The people living at that time will certainly not die until all these things happen.
The heaven and the earth, even if they perish, and your Lord is not deficient in them, a day on which the good will be in proportion to their deeds.
No one knows that day or hour. Not even the angels in heaven know. The Son does not know. Only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. So will it be when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage to them. They were doing all those things until the day that Noah entered the ark.
They did not know what would happen until the flood came and swept them all away. It will be just like that when the Son of Man comes.
There will be two men in a field. One will be taken and the other left.
You will have a woman kneading with a manual mill, they will take one and leave the other.
So fear, for you do not know on what day your Lord will come to you.
You have to understand something. Imagine that the homeowner knows the exact time the thief will arrive. In that case, he would maintain a vigilant watch over his house. He would not allow anyone to penetrate his home.
Therefore you also must be ready; for the Son of Man will come at an hour when you do not expect.
Imagine a master who put one of his servants in charge of the other servants in his house. The servant's job is to give them their food at the proper time. The master wants a faithful and wise servant for this.
It would be better for the servant if the master found him doing his work when the master returned.
What I am going to tell you is true. The master will put this servant in charge of everything he owns.
But what if this slave is wicked? What if he says to himself: My master is going to be away for a very long time.
If he begins to beat the other servants. And if he eats and drinks with those who drink excessively.
The master of that servant will come on a day when the servant does not expect him. He will return at an hour that the servant does not know.
Then his master will cut him into pieces and send him to the place where the flatterers go. There the people will weep and gnash their teeth.
The Story of the Ten Brides
This is an image of the kingdom of the heavens at that time. Ten maidens went out with their lamps to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were foolish. And five were wise.
The ignorant ones took their lamps but did not take olive oil.
The wise ones took oil in vessels with their lamps.
The groom didn't come for quite some time. So the people with him got very impatient and fell asleep.
In the middle of the night, someone shouted: "Here is the groom! Come to meet him!"
Then all the girls woke up and prepared their lamps.
The fools said to the wise, "Give us some of your oil. For our lamps are going out."
"'No,' they replied. 'There may not be enough for all of us. Instead, go to those who are selling the oil. Buy some for yourselves.'"
So they went to buy oil. But while they were on their way, the bridegroom arrived. And the brides who were ready with him entered the dining room. Then she closed the door.
After that, the other girls came too. "Sir! Sir!" they said. "Open the door for us!"
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you is true. I do not know you."
Therefore, guard yourselves, so that three of you are not in the same house, unless one of them dies.
Here is another parable about the Kingdom of Heaven. A man was going on a journey. He called his servants and gave them his money to keep.
Here is the translation: He gave $10,000 to one person. He gave $4,000 to another person. And he gave $2,000 to the third person. The man gave each servant the amount of money that he knew the servant could take care of. Then he went on his trip.
The server that received $10,000 immediately went and started investing his money. He earned another $10,000.
The one who won 4000 dirhams won another 4000 dirhams.
But the man who received 2000 dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. And he hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of these servants returned. He wanted to collect all the money that they had earned.
The man who received 10 thousand dollars brought the other 10 thousand dollars. He said: 'Sir, you entrusted me with 10 thousand dollars. Look, I earned another 10 thousand dollars.
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things; I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share your master's happiness!"
The man who came with about 4000 dollars, said, 'Sir, you entrusted me with 4000 dollars. Look, I have earned another 4000 dollars.'
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things; I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share your master's happiness!"
Then the man who had received the 2000 dollars came. He said, "Sir, I knew you were a hard man. You reap where you did not sow. You gather crops where you did not plant seed."
I was afraid. I went out and hid $2000 for you in the ground. Look, here is what is yours.
His master answered, "You wicked, lazy servant! Did you not know that I harvest where I did not sow, and that I gather where I did not scatter?
Well, you should have put my money in the bank. When I came back, I would have received it with interest.
"Then his master ordered the other servants, 'Take his 2000 dollars. Give it to the one who has 20000 dollars.'
To those who have, more will be given. They will have more than enough. And what about those who have nothing? Even what little they have will be taken away from them.
Cast out this worthless servant. There in the darkness, people will weep and grind their teeth.
Then the Son of Man will come in his glory. And he will send his angels with a great trumpet call, and they will gather his elect from the four winds, from one end of heaven to the other.
All nations will gather before him. He will separate people into two groups. It will be like a shepherd separating sheep from goats.
Others put the sheep on their right and the goats on their left.
"Then the king speaks to those on his right, saying, 'My father has blessed you. Come, take what is yours. It is the kingdom that was prepared for you from the creation of the world.'"
I was hungry. And you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty. And you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger. And you invited me in.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
"Then those who believed, from among them, will come and they will say, 'O our Lord, when did we fall upon you thirsty, so you gave us to drink, and when did we afflict you with pain, so you honored us?'"
When did we see you as a stranger and invite you in? When did we see you in need of clothes and give them to you?
When have we ever seen you sick or in prison and we went to visit you?
The man will say, 'The Lord will tell you, I am not skilled in speaking, for what I am skilled in is doing what is for one of these younger brothers of mine for my sake.'"
"Then he will say to those on his left, 'Cursed are those! Guide them from the light of the fire that blazes and belongs to Satan and his kingdom.'"
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger. But you did not invite me in. I needed clothes. But you did not give me anything. I was sick and in prison. But you did not care for me.
And they say, "Our Lord, when have we ever stood in need of You, hungry, and then fed You; or thirsty, and then quenched Your thirst; or a stranger, and then found You; or naked, and then clothed You; or sick, and then healed You; or imprisoned, and then freed You?"
He will answer: "The very thing that I tell you is true. Whatever you did not do for one of these least, you did not do for me."
Then they will flee from it, and those who flee will not prosper. And those are the ones who believed in Our signs, and We wrote down with them the covenant that they are believers.
The plan to kill Jesus.
Jesus finished saying all these things. Then he said to his disciples:
As you know, Easter is two days away. The Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified on the cross.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled in the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest.
They planned to arrest Jesus cunningly. They wanted to kill him.
But not during the feast, they said. People might make trouble, a woman might anoint Jesus with perfume.
Jesus was in the house of Bethany. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with a special, very expensive perfume bottle sealed shut. She poured the perfume on his head while he was at the table.
When the students saw this, they became angry. "Why is there such waste?" they asked.
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been given to the poor.
Jesus knowing this, said to them, "Why do you distress this woman? For she has done a beautiful thing to me."
Tomorrow, by my Lord, it will be with you, the poor. But it will not be with you always.
She poured perfume on my body to prepare me for burial.
What I am about to tell you is true. What I have done will be spoken of wherever this good news is preached throughout the world. It will be spoken of in memory of her." Judas agrees to hand Jesus over.
One of the twelve went to the chief priests. His name was Judas Iscariot.
He asked, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" So they counted out thirty pieces of silver for him.
Since then, Judas had been watching for an opportune time to hand Jesus over to them. The Last Supper.
This was the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread. The disciples came to Jesus. They asked, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover?"
Go to a certain man in the city. Tell him, 'The teacher says: "My hour has come near. I will go to celebrate the Passover at your house with my disciples."'"
So the disciples did what Jesus had instructed them to do. They prepared the Passover meal.
When evening came, Jesus was there with the twelve at the table.
While he was eating, he said, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. One of you will betray me to my enemies."
The disciples were very much grieved, and each of them began to say to him, "Is it I, Lord?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dipped his hand with me in the bowl, he will betray me."
The son of man will go away just as it is written about him. But how I dread the might of the one who will betray the son of man! It would have been better for him not to have been born.
It was Judas who was going to betray him. He said, "It is not I, Rabbi, is it?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is you."
While they were eating, Jesus took bread, and when He had given thanks, He broke it and gave it to the disciples, saying, "Take, eat; this is My body."
Then he took the cup. He thanked and gave it to them. He said, "Drink all of it from it."
This is the blood of the New Covenant. It is poured out to forgive the sins of many.
Here's what I say to you. From now on, I will never drink wine with you again until the day I drink it with you in my father's kingdom.
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus says that Peter will fail.
Jesus said to them, "In this very night, he will betray all of you for my sake. It is written: 'The Lord said: "I will strike down the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered."' (Zechariah 13:7)"
But after I am raised, I will go to Galilee before you.
Peter answered, "Everyone else may well abandon you. But I will never do so."
"What I am about to tell you is true," Jesus answered. "This very night before the rooster crows, you yourself will disown me three times."
But Peter said, "I will never die with you. " And he said, "I do not know what you are talking about. " And all the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus' prayer in Gethsemane
Then Jesus went out with his disciples to a place called Gethsemane. And he said to them, "Sit here, while I go over there and pray."
Peter took with him James and John, the sons of Zebedee, and he began to feel sorrow and distress.
Then he said to them, "My heart is deeply troubled. I feel the coming of death. Stay here and keep watch with me."
He walked a little further. Then he fell to his face on the ground. He prayed, saying, "Abba, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. But let it be as you will, not as I will."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. "Could you not watch with me for one hour?" he asked Peter.
Be vigilant and pray. Then you will not fall into sin when tempted. The spirit is willing. But the flesh is weak.
Jesus went a second time and prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from Me; yet not as I will, but as You will."
Then he came back. He found them sleeping again. They couldn't keep their eyes open.
So he left them and went away once more. For the third time he prayed the same thing.
Then he returned to the disciples. He said to them, "Are you sleeping and resting? Look at the green field and the doves. Don't you speak to them?"
Arise! Let us go! Behold, he whom they deliver up to me! Jesus is being arrested.
Jesus was speaking until John the Baptist came (one of the twelve disciples). He was with a large crowd carrying swords and sticks. He sent them to him, the chief priests and elders of the people.
Judah, who was going to betray Jesus, had arranged a signal with them. "The man I kiss, I will betray him," he said. "Seize him."
And so John went to Jesus at that hour. He said, "Peace be with you, Teacher!" And he kissed him.
Jesus answered, "Friend, do what you have come for." Then the men stepped forward. They grabbed Jesus and arrested him.
And behold, one of those who were with Jesus stretched out his hand and drew his sword and struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
"Put your sword back in its place," Jesus said to him. "For all who draw the sword will perish by the sword."
You think I can't ask my father for help? He will send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But how then are the prophecies fulfilled? They say that this must happen.
At that time, Jesus spoke to the crowd. He said, "Am I leading a rebellion against you by calling together an army of men armed with swords and spears? If that were my plan, would I have told you about it? Yet every day I was with you in the Temple courts, teaching the people. And you did not arrest me."
But all this was to fulfill the words of the prophets.' Then all his disciples deserted him and fled. Jesus to the High Priest's Council
They took Jesus, who had been arrested, to Caiaphas, the high priest. Now there were many scribes and elders gathered together.
It was not very far, Peter followed Jesus. He went straight to the courtyard of the high priest. He entered and sat with the guards to see what would happen.
The chief priests and the whole Sanhedrin were searching for something they could use against Jesus. They wanted to kill him.
But they found no evidence, although many false witnesses had appeared. Finally, two more witnesses appeared.
They said, "This man claimed, 'I can destroy God's house. And I can rebuild it from scratch in three days.'"
Then the high priest rose and said, "Jesus, can you not tell us what these men accuse you of?"
But Jesus remained silent. The high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God, tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
"Yes, it is as you say," Jesus answered. "But this is what I tell you all. In the coming days, you will see the Son of Man seated at the right hand of the Mighty One. And you will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the priests of the congregation approached and tore their robes. They said, "He has said a very evil thing against God! Why do we need more witnesses than this? You have heard him say this evil."
What are you going to say? They said: He died, he must die.
Then they licked his face. One of them punched him with his fist. Others slapped him.
They said, "Jesus, prophesy to us, who is it that struck you?" Peter says, "I don't know Jesus."
Peter was sitting in the courtyard. A servant girl came to him. "I too was with Jesus of Galilee," she said.
But in front of all these, Peter said that he is not. "I do not know about what you are talking about" he said.
Then he went out to the gate from which one enters the paradise. And another woman saw him from another door. And she said to the people: "This man was with Jesus the Nazarene."
And again he said it was not so. And with an oath he said, "I do not know the man!"
Soon, those who were standing there approached Peter. "You must be one of them," they said. "The way you talk shows it."
Then he began to translate the curses upon himself. He acknowledged them upon himself and said to them: "I do not know the man!" And so a cock then crowed.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said. "A cock will not crow," Jesus had told him. "Before it crows, you will have denied me three times." Peter went out and wept bitterly, his strength was gone.
Judah hangs himself.
That was early in the morning. All the chief priests and the elders of the people decided to crucify Jesus.
They tied his hands and dragged him. Then they handed him over to Pilate, who was the governor.
They saw the one who had handed him over, for they knew that he had spoken of him. When Jesus came and spoke to them, they said to him, "Rabbi" (which is to say, when interpreted, Teacher), "where have you been?" He said to them, "I must preach the good news of the kingdom of God to the other cities also; for I was sent for this purpose." And he went from one town and village to another, preaching and bringing the good news of the kingdom of God.
I made a mistake," he said. "I handed over an innocent man." "What does it matter to us?" they answered. "This is your problem."
Then Yohanna threw the manna in the mosque and left. Then he left and committed suicide.
The priests of the temple picked up the coins. "We have committed a crime," they said. "The placing of this money in the temple coffer is blood money. It is the price paid for a man's death."
They decided to use the money to buy a burial field. People coming from other countries will be buried there.
This is why it was called the Field of Blood until this day.
Then the words of Jeremiah the prophet appeared. He said: "They have taken the forty pieces of silver from the pot, the price at which they valued him from the sons of Israel."
They used the coins to buy a potter's field, as the Lord had instructed me." (Zechariah 11:12,13; Mark 1:1-13; 6:6-9) Jesus is brought before Pilate
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the high priest and the elders accused him, he gave no answer.
Then Pilate asked him, saying, "Don't you hear how many things they testify against you?"
But Jesus answered not a word to any of the charges. The governor was exceedingly astonished.
It was the custom of the governor at Passover to release a single prisoner. The people could choose whom they wanted.
In that situation, they were holding a famous prisoner called Barrabassa.
When Pilate had gathered the crowd, he said to them, "Which one do you want me to release for you, Barabbas or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were envious. He knew that this was why they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judge's seat, his wife sent him a message. "Don't do anything with that man," she said. "He is innocent. I suffered a lot in today's dream because of this man."
But the chief priests and the elders persuaded the crowd to ask for Barabbas and to have Jesus executed.
"Which of the two do you want me to release?" asked the governor. "Barabbas" they answered.
So what shall I do then with Jesus who is called the Messiah? asked Pilate. They all answered together: Crucify him.
Why? What has he done? asked Pilate. But they cried out all the louder, Crucify him!
Pilate saw that this was going nowhere. In fact, the crowd was starting to get angry. So he washed his hands in front of them, saying, "I will not be responsible for this man's death. You are the ones responsible!"
Everyone answered: "We and our children will bear the responsibility for his death!"
Pilate released Baruch. But he flogged Jesus. Then he handed him over to be crucified on the cross. The guards mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Praetorium. And all the other soldiers gathered around him.
They removed his clothes and dressed him in a shiny suit.
Then they twisted the thorns together to make a crown. They put it on his head. They put a stick in his right hand. Then they knelt down in front of him and laughed at him. "We honor you, King of the Jews!" they said.
They reviled him. He struck him repeatedly and repeatedly on the head with the scourge.
After they ridiculed him, they stripped him of his robe. They returned his clothing to him. Then they led him away to be crucified on a cross. The crucifixion of Jesus.
As they were going out of the city, they met a man from Cyrene. His name was Simon. They forced him to carry the cross.
They came to a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means "the place of the skull."
There they mixed the wine with types of bitter spices and gave it to Jesus to drink. And after he tasted it, he refused to drink it.
When they crucified him, they divided his garments by casting lots.
They sat there guarding him.
They placed above his head the written accusation against him. It said: "This is Jesus, the King of the Jews."
Two thieves were crucified with him. One on his right and one on his left.
They are the ones who passed by, shouting at Jesus and mocking him. And they shake their heads.
And he said, "So then you will destroy the temple and raise it again in three days? Save yourself! Come down from the cross, if you are the Son of God!"
Likewise, the chief priests, the teachers of the law and the elders mocked him.
"They saved the others," they said. "But he cannot save himself! He is the king of Israel! Let him come down from the cross now! Then we will believe in him."
He trusts in God. Let God save him now if he wants to. It is he who said: "I am the Son of God."
Even the thieves who were crucified with Jesus mocked him. Jesus' death
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered the entire earth.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out loudly, saying, "Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?" This means "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those who stood by heard Jesus calling out. They said, "He is calling Elijah."
Immediately one of them ran to the market, took a sponge, and filled it with vinegar on a reed. He offered it to Jesus to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him alone. Let's see if Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
And at that moment, the veil of the mosque was torn from above until below. And the earth rumbled. And the rocks were split.
Graves were opened. The bodies of many saints who had died were brought back to life.
They came out of the tombs. After Jesus ascended into heaven, they entered the holy city. There they appeared to many people.
The Roman centurion and Jesus' guard saw the earthquake and what happened. They were terrified. "Certainly this was the Son of God!" they exclaimed.
Not so far away, there were many women watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was one of them. Mary, the mother of James and Joseph, was there also. And also the mother of the sons of Zebedee. Jesus was buried
When evening came, a wealthy man from Arimathea came. His name was Joseph. He had become a follower of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate ordered that he be given it.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean piece of cloth.
He placed it in his new tomb which he had carved out of the rock. And he pushed a large stone in front of the entrance to the tomb. Then he went away.
Mary Magdalene and the other woman were sitting there before the tomb. The guards at the tomb
The next day was the one following the preparation day. The chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
"Sir," they said, "we remember something a liar said while he was alive. He claimed, 'After three days, I will return anew.'"
Give us an order for them to make the tomb secure until the third day. Otherwise, his disciples may come and steal the body. Then they will go and tell the people that Jesus has been raised from the dead. And this last lie will be worse than the first.
"Take some guards with you," Pilate answered. "Go. Make the tomb as secure as possible."
So they went and made the grave secure. They placed a seal on the stone and put some guards on the role.
Jesus ascends to heaven.
Saturday had ended. Dawn had broken on the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other woman went to check on the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. The Angel of the Lord came down from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. The stone rolled back and sat on it.
His body was shining like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were trembling and almost falling down like dead men from their intense fear of him.
The angel said to the woman, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus who was crucified."
There is no! Help him, as he said! Bring him and see the place where he was hiding.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples, 'He has been raised from the dead. He is going ahead of you to Galilee. There you will see him.' This is what I told you.'
The women rushed out of the tomb. They were afraid, but they were filled with joy. They ran to tell the disciples.
Then Jesus (peace be upon him) came and said, "Peace be upon you." So they came to him, and they took him by his legs, and they sought forgiveness for him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me." The guards proceed over the senior priests.
While the women were on their way, some of the guards entered the city. They reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the high priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave the soldiers a large sum of money.
They told the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the Governor hears this report, we will push it. This will keep you out of trouble.
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story has spread all over among the Jews to this day. Jesus' last instructions to his disciples
Then the eleventh disciple went to Galilee. So they went to the mountain to which Jesus had commanded them to go.
When they saw him, they bowed down to him. But some had their doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Go then and make disciples of all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey all that I have commanded you. And you can be sure that I will be with you always, to the end.
John the Baptist paves the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote: "I will send my messenger before you. He will prepare your way" (Malachi 3:1)
A messenger is calling in the desert: "Make straight in the wilderness a highway for our God. Make smooth in the desert a road for our God." (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He baptized people in the desert. He also preached that people should be baptized and repent of their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the country folk of Judah came out to him, and all the people of Jerusalem; and when they confessed their sins, he baptized them in the river Jordan.
John was wearing clothes made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. He was eating locusts and wild honey.
Here is what John used to preach. "After me will come one who is mightier than I. I am not good enough to stoop down and untie his sandals."
I will wash you with water. But he who is washing you is anointing you with the Holy Spirit. Jesus also carries the anointing and the temptation.
In those days Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee, and was baptized by John in the Jordan.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. And at that moment, he saw the heavens being torn open. And he saw the Holy Spirit descending on him like a dove.
He spoke to him, a voice from heaven. It said, "You are my son, and I love you. I am pleased with you."
And the Holy Spirit departed from Jesus and sent him to the wilderness.
He stayed in the desert for 40 days. There, the devil tempted him. Wild animals did not harm him. Angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was imprisoned, Jesus went into Galilee, preaching the gospel of God.
"It is time," he said. "The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe in the good news!"
Jesus was walking one day on the shores of the Sea of Galilee. And there he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting their net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets and followed him immediately.
Then Jesus walked a little way from where he was. And behold, he was looking at Jacob, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat mending their nets.
And immediately he called out to them, "Stop! Leave the dead to bury their dead!" And leaving their father, Zebedee, in the boat with the hired men, they followed Jesus. Jesus Commands an Evil Spirit
Jesus came, and those who were with him, to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath day had come, he entered into the synagogue and began to teach.
People were amazed at his teachings. He taught them with authority. He did not speak as the teachers of the law.
A man from the church came, and he was enchanted. And he said
What is your purpose with us, Jesus of Nazareth? Did you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!
Be quiet, Jesus said firmly. Get out of him.
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him, screaming.
The people were amazed by him. They said on that day, "This is nothing but a human being like us. He eats foods and drinks drinks. If he is anything, he is only a human being like us, reminding and alerting us. Perhaps, if he is anything, he is only correct."
The news about Jesus spread rapidly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus and those who were with him left the temple. Then they went immediately with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand, and helped her stand up. The fever left her body. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, people brought to Jesus all the sick. They also brought all those who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They suffered from all kinds of diseases. He also cast out many demons. But he did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prayed in a quiet place.
It was early dawn and still dark. Jesus arose and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out: "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to another place. I want to go to the neighboring villages. I have to preach there too. That is why I came."
So he traveled throughout all of Galilee. And he was preaching in their synagogues. And he was also saving those possessed, even those possessed by demons. Jesus satisfies a man with sores.
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt down and asked Jesus. He said, "If you are willing to make me 'clean,' you can do it."
Jesus was very disturbing. He reached out his hand and touched the man. "I am ready to do that," he said. "Be 'clean'!
The illness left him immediately. He has recovered.
Jesus immediately sent the man away. He gave the man a strong warning.
Don't tell this to anyone, he said. Go and present yourself to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be proof to the priest and the people that you are pure.
But the man went out and began to talk at once. And he spread the news to everyone. So Jesus no longer entered a city publicly. And he remained out in deserted places. But the people were still coming to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. And the people heard that he had come to his house.
Many people gathered until there was no more room. There was not even room outside the door. And Krazos preached the word to them.
Four of the newcomers were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't get him close enough to Jesus because of the crowd. So they dug a hole through the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man down through it on a mat.
Jesus saw their faith. And he said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some law professors were sitting there. They were thinking,
Who is this person talking like that? They are saying some really bad things! Only God will judge the sins.
Immediately Jesus knew in his spirit what they were thinking in their hearts. And he said to them, "Why are you thinking these things in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise, take up your prayer rug and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
I tell you, he said, my people, take your Quran and go home.
The man stood up and took his mat. Then he walked away, while everyone was watching. Everyone was amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Jesus went out again beside the Sea of Galilee. A great multitude came to him. He began to teach them.
As he was traveling, he saw Lazarus, the son of Eleazar. Lazarus was sitting at the place for throwing away. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." So Lazarus rose up and followed him.
Later, Jesus was eating dinner at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that followed Jesus.
There were some Pharisee teachers of the law. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard that. He said to them: "Those who are healthy do not need a physician, but those who are sick. I did not come to call the righteous to repentance, but the sick to repentance." Jesus asks about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. Some people came to Jesus. They said to him, "John's disciples are fasting. The disciples of the Pharisees are fasting. But your disciples are not fasting. Why are they not fasting?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them."
But comes the time when the bridegroom will be taken from them. In that day they shall go out unto the marriage, but shall not eat.
There is no one who can sew a new piece onto old cloth. Because that would cause the new piece to tear from the old side. And further widen the breach.
No one puts new wine into old wineskins. If he does, the wine will burst the skins, and both the wine and the skins will be ruined. No, people pour new wine into new wineskins. Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath Jesus was walking with his disciples in the wheat fields. And the disciples began to pluck some of the wheat spikes.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, they are doing deeds on the Sabbath, and why do they not teach your disciples to stop doing this?"
He answered, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was in the time of Abiathar the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate from the holy bread. Eating it was restricted to the priests only. David also gave some of it to his men.
Then Jesus said to them: 'The Sabbath was made for man's sake, and not man for the Sabbath's sake.'
So the Son of Man is lord even over his own day.
Healing on Saturday
Jesus entered the temple one day. There was a man with a weak, bent hand.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They watched him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man who had a withered and shriveled hand. He said to him, "Stand up in front of everyone."
Then Jesus said to them: "What does the law say we must do in the Sabbath day? To do good or evil? To save life or to destroy it?" And no one answered him.
Jesus saw the people and was angry. He was uncomfortable because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He did so, and his hand was restored as good as new.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus. For they had been sent to observe Jesus and report to the Pharisees and the Herodians. And the plot to kill Jesus was forming. Crowds were following Jesus.
Jesus set out with his disciples for the Sea of Galilee. Great crowds followed him from Galilee.
The people heard about all the things Jesus was doing. Many people came to him. They were coming from Judea, and Jerusalem, and Idumea. They were coming from the territories east of the River Jordan. And they were coming from the area around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a small boat for him, where he could sit, so that the people would not crowd him.
Jesus healed many people. Therefore, the sick were pushing forward to touch him.
When the people saw him possessed by evil spirits, they fell to the ground. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed twelve of them and named them apostles. And from that time they were with him. And he sent them also to preach to the people.
They will have the authority to expel demons.
Jesus said to the ten, "Go to the sea, and cast a net for a catch."
There was James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And there were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there was also Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
John the Apostle was one of them. He was the one who went to hand Jesus over to his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. Another crowd gathered. The crowd was so large that Jesus and his disciples could not eat.
His family heard about this. So they went to take care of it. They said, "He's gone off the deep end."
There were some teachers of the law. They came down from Jerusalem. "He is controlled by Beelzebul!" they said. "He is driving out demons by the power of the chief demon."
Jesus said to them, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights itself, it will not stand.
If the family disperses, it will not stand.
